        "# Unit 1: Foundations of Statistical Learning\n",
        "## Main Topics\n",
        "- What is statistical learning?\n",
        "- Supervised vs unsupervised learning\n",
        "- Parametric vs nonparametric methods\n",
        "- Prediction vs explanation\n",
        "- Bias--variance tradeoff\n",
        "\n",
        "## Teaching Goals\n",
        "Students should understand that the main goal of statistical learning is **good prediction on new data**, not simply fitting the observed sample as closely as possible.\n",
        "\n",
        "## Suggested Emphasis\n",
        "- Introduce the overall modeling problem first.\n",
        "- Use simple real-world examples such as house prices, medical diagnosis, or spam filtering.\n",
        "- Make overfitting a central theme from the beginning.\n",
        "\n",
        "# Unit 2: Linear Regression as a Baseline\n",
        "## Main Topics\n",
        "- Linear regression as a prediction tool\n",
        "- Interpreting fitted relationships\n",
        "- Strengths and weaknesses of linear models\n",
        "- Linear models as a baseline for comparison\n",
        "\n",
        "## Teaching Goals\n",
        "Students should see linear regression as the **starting point** of predictive modeling: simple, interpretable, and useful, but limited in flexibility.\n",
        "\n",
        "## Suggested Emphasis\n",
        "- Present linear regression mainly as a predictive model.\n",
        "- Keep inference light unless needed for context.\n",
        "- Highlight when linear models fail to capture nonlinear structure.\n",
        "\n",
        "# Unit 3: Nonparametric Regression\n",
        "## Main Topics\n",
        "- Flexible regression methods\n",
        "- K-nearest neighbors regression\n",
        "- Smoothers and local fitting ideas\n",
        "- Model flexibility and overfitting\n",
        "\n",
        "## Teaching Goals\n",
        "Students should learn that more flexible models can better capture complicated patterns, but at the cost of increased variance.\n",
        "## Suggested Emphasis\n",
        "- Connect this unit directly to bias--variance tradeoff.\n",
        "- Compare flexible and inflexible models visually.\n",
        "- Use plots to show underfitting and overfitting.\n",
        "\n",
        "# Unit 4: Classification\n",
        "## Main Topics\n",
        "- Classification as a prediction problem\n",
        "- Logistic regression\n",
        "- Decision boundaries\n",
        "- K-nearest neighbors classification\n",
        "\n",
        "## Teaching Goals\n",
        "Students should understand that classification uses the same modeling logic as regression, but with categorical outcomes.\n",
        "## Suggested Emphasis\n",
        "- Stress the difference between predicting a class and predicting a numeric response.\n",
        "- Introduce class probabilities early.\n",
        "- Use confusion matrices and classification error as evaluation tools.\n",
        "\n",
