        "## Prediction, Inference, and Causality\n",
        "\n",
        "In statistics and data science, **prediction**, **statistical inference**, and **causal inference** are three fundamental tasks.  \n",
        "They are **not hierarchical**, but they differ in goals, assumptions, and methodologies.\n",
        "\n",
        "---\n",
        "\n",
        "### 1. Prediction\n",
        "\n",
        "**Goal:** Accurately predict the outcome $Y$ given predictors $X$.\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\hat f(x) \\approx \\mathbb{E}[Y \\mid X = x]\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "- Focus: **out-of-sample performance** (test error, cross-validation)\n",
        "- Assumptions: relatively weak\n",
        "- Methods: Ridge, Lasso, trees, boosting, neural networks\n",
        "\n",
        "**Key idea:**  \n",
        "Prediction captures patterns in the data and is validated empirically by generalization.\n",
        "\n",
        "---\n",
        "\n",
        "### 2. Statistical Inference\n",
        "\n",
        "**Goal:** Draw conclusions about relationships or parameters in the population.\n",
        "\n",
        "Examples:\n",
        "- Is $\\beta_j = 0$?\n",
        "- What is the confidence interval for $\\beta_j$?\n",
        "\n",
        "- Focus: **uncertainty quantification**\n",
        "- Requires: **model assumptions** (linearity, independence, etc.)\n",
        "- Tools: standard errors, p-values, confidence intervals\n",
        "\n",
        "**Key idea:**  \n",
        "Inference generalizes from sample to population under specified assumptions.\n",
        "\n",
        "---\n",
        "\n",
        "### 3. Causal Inference\n",
        "\n",
        "**Goal:** Determine the effect of interventions.\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\mathbb{E}[Y \\mid do(X = x)]\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "- Question: *What happens if we change $X$?*\n",
        "- Requires: **causal structure or experimental design**\n",
        "  - Randomized experiments (RCT)\n",
        "  - Instrumental variables (IV)\n",
        "  - Difference-in-differences (DiD)\n",
        "\n",
        "**Key idea:**  \n",
        "Causal inference identifies how the data-generating mechanism responds to interventions.\n",
        "\n",
        "---\n",
        "\n",
        "### 4. Relationship and Differences\n",
        "\n",
        "Although distinct, the three tasks can be viewed as requiring **increasing levels of structure and information**:\n",
        "\n",
        "| Task        | Required Information        | Validation Method        | Question Answered        |\n",
        "|------------|----------------------------|--------------------------|--------------------------|\n",
        "| Prediction | Data distribution          | Test error / CV          | What will happen?        |\n",
        "| Inference  | Model + assumptions        | Theory + diagnostics     | Is there a relationship? |\n",
        "| Causality  | Structure + identification | Design + assumptions     | What if we intervene?    |\n",
        "\n",
        "---\n",
        "\n",
        "### 5. Why Causality Is Harder\n",
        "\n",
        "Causal inference is more difficult because:\n",
        "\n",
        "1. **Data alone is insufficient**\n",
        "\n",
        "   $$\n",
        "   P(Y, X) \\neq P(Y \\mid do(X))\n",
        "   $$\n",
        "\n",
        "2. **Confounding variables** may distort relationships\n",
        "\n",
        "3. **Counterfactuals are unobservable**\n",
        "\n",
        "   We cannot observe both outcomes under different interventions for the same unit.\n",
        "\n",
        "---\n",
        "\n",
        "### 6. Interaction Between the Three\n",
        "\n",
        "- Predictive models can help identify useful variables or nonlinear structure.\n",
        "- Inferential models provide interpretable summaries and uncertainty quantification.\n",
        "- Causal analysis requires additional assumptions beyond both prediction and inference.\n",
        "\n",
        "**Important:**\n",
        "> Good prediction does not imply valid inference or causal conclusions.  \n",
        "> Significant associations do not imply causation.\n",
        "\n",
        "---\n",
        "\n",
        "### 7. Summary\n",
        "\n",
        "> Prediction, inference, and causality are complementary but fundamentally different statistical tasks.  \n",
        "> Each requires its own assumptions, validation criteria, and methodology.\n",
        "\n",
        "**One-line intuition:**\n",
        "- Prediction: *What will happen?*  \n",
        "- Inference: *Is there a relationship?*  \n",
        "- Causality: *What happens if we intervene?*\n",
        "\n",
        "**Final takeaway:**\n",
        "> Prediction is validated by data, inference is justified by assumptions, and causality requires design.\n",
        "\n",
