        "\n",
        ":contentReference[oaicite:2]{index=2}\n",
        "\n",
        "Thus precision depends not only on recall, but also on:\n",
        "\n",
        "- class prevalence\n",
        "- prediction frequency\n",
        "\n",
        "This explains why precision can collapse in highly imbalanced datasets.\n",
        "\n",
        "---\n",
        "\n",
        "# Class Imbalance\n",
        "\n",
        "Suppose only 1% of observations are positive.\n",
        "\n",
        "Even a classifier with high recall may have poor precision.\n",
        "\n",
        "For example:\n",
        "\n",
        "- 100 true positives\n",
        "- 900 false positives\n",
        "\n",
        "Then:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\text{Recall}\n",
        "=\n",
        "1\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "but:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\text{Precision}\n",
        "=\n",
        "\\frac{100}{1000}\n",
        "=\n",
        "0.1.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Thus most predicted positives are incorrect.\n",
        "\n",
        "This is why accuracy alone becomes misleading under class imbalance.\n",
        "\n",
        "---\n",
        "\n",
        "# Thresholds and Decision Rules\n",
        "\n",
        "Most classifiers produce scores or probabilities:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "p(x)=P(Y=1\\mid x).\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "A final classification requires a threshold:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\hat Y=\n",
        "\\begin{cases}\n",
        "1 & p(x)>c\\\\\n",
        "0 & p(x)\\le c\n",
        "\\end{cases}\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "where $c$ is the classification threshold.\n",
        "\n",
        "Changing the threshold changes precision and recall.\n",
        "\n",
        "---\n",
        "\n",
        "# Precision-Recall Tradeoff\n",
        "\n",
        "Suppose we lower the threshold.\n",
        "\n",
        "Then the classifier predicts positive more aggressively.\n",
        "\n",
        "This usually causes:\n",
        "\n",
        "- recall to increase\n",
        "- precision to decrease\n",
        "\n",
        "because:\n",
        "\n",
        "- more true positives are detected\n",
        "- but more false positives are also generated\n",
        "\n",
        "Conversely, raising the threshold produces:\n",
        "\n",
        "- higher precision\n",
        "- lower recall\n",
        "\n",
        "Thus precision and recall naturally trade off against each other.\n",
        "\n",
        "---\n",
        "\n",
        "# Statistical Decision Theory\n",
        "\n",
        "This tradeoff is fundamentally a decision-theoretic problem.\n",
        "\n",
        "Classification is not merely about finding the truth.\n",
        "\n",
        "Instead, it is about:\n",
        "\n",
        "> making decisions under uncertainty while balancing different error costs.\n",
        "\n",
        "Different applications prioritize different errors.\n",
        "\n",
        "---\n",
        "\n",
        "# Medical Screening Example\n",
        "\n",
        "Suppose we are screening for cancer.\n",
        "\n",
        "Missing a cancer patient may be extremely costly.\n",
        "\n",
        "Thus false negatives are dangerous.\n",
        "\n",
        "Therefore we prioritize:\n",
        "\n",
        "```text\n",
        "high recall\n",
        "```\n",
        "\n",
        "Even if this creates more false positives.\n",
        "\n",
        "Extra medical testing may be acceptable if it reduces missed diagnoses.\n",
        "\n",
        "---\n",
        "\n",
        "# Spam Filtering Example\n",
        "\n",
        "Now consider spam detection.\n",
        "\n",
        "If precision is low, many legitimate emails will be flagged as spam.\n",
        "\n",
        "Users may become frustrated.\n",
        "\n",
        "Thus precision becomes more important.\n",
        "\n",
        "This illustrates that the optimal tradeoff depends on the application.\n",
        "\n",
        "---\n",
        "\n",
        "# Precision, Recall, and the Bias-Variance Tradeoff\n",
        "\n",
        "Precision and recall are also connected to the bias-variance tradeoff.\n",
        "\n",
        "Suppose a classifier is extremely conservative.\n",
        "\n",
        "It predicts positive only when highly certain.\n",
        "\n",
        "Then:\n",
        "\n",
        "- precision may be high\n",
        "- recall may be low\n",
        "\n",
        "This resembles a high-bias classifier.\n",
        "\n",
        "The classifier avoids risky decisions.\n",
        "\n",
        "---\n",
        "\n",
        "# High Variance and Aggressive Classification\n",
        "\n",
        "Now suppose the classifier aggressively predicts positives.\n",
        "\n",
        "Then:\n",
        "\n",
        "- recall may increase\n",
        "- precision may decrease\n",
        "\n",
        "because the classifier begins fitting more subtle patterns.\n",
        "\n",
        "This resembles lower bias but higher variance.\n",
        "\n",
        "The classifier becomes more flexible and sensitive, but also more unstable.\n",
        "\n",
        "---\n",
        "\n",
        "# Thresholds as Complexity Control\n",
        "\n",
        "Changing the classification threshold can itself be viewed as controlling effective model complexity.\n",
        "\n",
        "A very low threshold creates:\n",
        "\n",
        "```text\n",
        "aggressive positive prediction\n",
        "```\n",
        "\n",
        "A very high threshold creates:\n",
        "\n",
        "```text\n",
        "conservative positive prediction\n",
        "```\n",
        "\n",
        "Thus threshold selection acts like a bias-variance tuning mechanism.\n",
        "\n",
        "---\n",
        "\n",
        "# Example: Logistic Regression\n",
        "\n",
        "Suppose logistic regression estimates:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "P(Y=1\\mid x).\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "If we classify positive whenever:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "P(Y=1\\mid x)>0.5,\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "then the classifier behaves moderately.\n",
        "\n",
        "If we instead use:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "P(Y=1\\mid x)>0.1,\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "the classifier becomes far more aggressive.\n",
        "\n",
        "This usually increases recall but decreases precision.\n",
        "\n",
        "---\n",
        "\n",
        "# Precision-Recall Curves\n",
        "\n",
        "A precision-recall curve shows how precision changes as recall changes across thresholds.\n",
        "\n",
        "This curve is especially important for imbalanced datasets.\n",
        "\n",
        "Unlike ROC curves, precision-recall curves directly reflect the quality of positive predictions.\n",
        "\n",
        "---\n",
        "\n",
        "# F1 Score\n",
        "\n",
        "Sometimes we want to balance precision and recall simultaneously.\n",
        "\n",
        "The F1 score is:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "F1\n",
        "=\n",
        "\\frac{2PR}{P+R},\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "where:\n",
        "\n",
        "- $P$ = precision\n",
        "- $R$ = recall\n",
        "\n",
        "This is the harmonic mean of precision and recall.\n",
        "\n",
        "---\n",
        "\n",
        "# Why Harmonic Mean?\n",
        "\n",
        "The harmonic mean heavily penalizes imbalance.\n",
        "\n",
        "For example:\n",
        "\n",
        "- precision = 1\n",
        "- recall = 0\n",
        "\n",
        "then:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "F1=0.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Thus F1 favors classifiers that balance both quantities.\n",
        "\n",
        "---\n",
        "\n",
        "# Precision, Recall, and Model Flexibility\n",
        "\n",
        "Different models naturally produce different precision-recall behaviors.\n",
        "\n",
        "Highly flexible models:\n",
        "\n",
        "- deeper trees\n",
        "- boosting\n",
        "- neural networks\n",
        "\n",
        "may achieve higher recall by detecting subtle patterns.\n",
        "\n",
        "However, they may also generate more false positives.\n",
        "\n",
        "Simpler models:\n",
        "\n",
        "- linear models\n",
        "- regularized models\n",
        "\n",
        "may produce fewer false positives but miss weaker signals.\n",
        "\n",
        "Thus precision-recall behavior is deeply tied to model complexity.\n",
        "\n",
        "---\n",
        "\n",
        "# Boosting and Recall\n",
        "\n",
        "Boosting methods such as AdaBoost and XGBoost often aggressively improve recall.\n",
        "\n",
        "Boosting repeatedly focuses on difficult observations.\n",
        "\n",
        "As a result, boosting tends to:\n",
        "\n",
        "- reduce false negatives\n",
        "- improve detection power\n",
        "\n",
        "However, aggressive boosting may also increase false positives if the model begins fitting noise.\n",
        "\n",
        "Thus boosting often navigates a delicate precision-recall tradeoff.\n",
        "\n",
        "---\n",
        "\n",
        "# Hypothesis Testing Analogy\n",
        "\n",
        "The connection between classification and hypothesis testing is profound.\n",
        "\n",
        "Classification threshold selection resembles choosing a significance level.\n",
        "\n",
        "For example:\n",
        "\n",
        "- lower threshold\n",
        "  - easier to reject $H_0$\n",
        "  - higher recall\n",
        "  - more false positives\n",
        "\n",
        "- higher threshold\n",
        "  - harder to reject $H_0$\n",
        "  - lower recall\n",
        "  - fewer false positives\n",
        "\n",
        "This parallels:\n",
        "\n",
        "- liberal tests\n",
        "- conservative tests\n",
        "\n",
        "in classical hypothesis testing.\n",
        "\n",
        "---\n",
        "\n",
        "# Unified Statistical View\n",
        "\n",
        "We may summarize the connections as follows.\n",
        "\n",
        "| Classification | Hypothesis Testing |\n",
        "|---|---|\n",
        "| Positive prediction | Reject $H_0$ |\n",
        "| Negative prediction | Fail to reject $H_0$ |\n",
        "| False positive | Type I error |\n",
        "| False negative | Type II error |\n",
        "| Recall | Power |\n",
        "| Precision | Reliability of discoveries |\n",
        "\n",
        "This unified viewpoint connects machine learning classification directly to classical statistics.\n",
        "\n",
        "---\n",
        "\n",
        "# Final Remarks\n",
        "\n",
        "Precision and recall are not merely machine learning metrics.\n",
        "\n",
        "They arise naturally from:\n",
        "\n",
        "- statistical decision theory\n",
        "- hypothesis testing\n",
        "- uncertainty quantification\n",
        "- asymmetric loss\n",
        "- bias-variance tradeoffs\n",
        "\n",
        "Recall measures the ability to detect true signals.\n",
        "\n",
        "Precision measures the reliability of positive discoveries.\n",
        "\n",
        "Different applications require different balances between these quantities.\n",
        "\n",
        "Ultimately, classification is not simply about maximizing accuracy.\n",
        "\n",
        "It is about making statistically meaningful decisions under uncertainty while balancing competing risks. -->\n"
